---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import flagNote from "../../assets/images/project-notes/flagNote.png";
import flagsIndicators from "../../assets/images/project-notes/flagsIndicators.png"
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
        title="Collaborative flags"
        description="I turned a predefined flagging request into an assignable notes system with deadlines and
                    notifications, reusing a familiar pattern instead of introducing a separate task tool."
>
    <main>
        <article class="case-study project-note">
            <header class="case-study__header">
                <p class="case-study__type">Product decision</p>

                <h1>Turning flags into collaborative tasks</h1>

                <p class="case-study__lead">
                    When DOAJ asked for a way to flag a record for another editor, I identified that the
                    proposed interaction separated the task from the information needed to complete it. I
                    redesigned the feature around actionable notes, bringing context, responsibility and
                    deadlines into one familiar pattern.
                </p>
            </header>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Finding the task behind the flag</h2>

                <p>
                    The original proposal allowed a Managing Editor to flag a journal, application or
                    update request for another user. The flag would identify who needed to act, while a
                    separate note would explain what they needed to do.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This answered an important operational need: making sure reminders and tasks reached
                    the right person at the right time, without relying on paper notes or personal
                    calendars. Earlier user interviews had also repeatedly identified better support for
                    collaborative work as a priority.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Separating the flag from its explanation, however, created several problems. Users
                    would have to perform two actions for a single task, it might not remain clear which
                    note belonged to the flag, and newer notes could push the relevant information further
                    down the list. A separate flag interface would also add more controls to an already
                    crowded record layout.
                </p>
            </section>


            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Making an existing note actionable</h2>

                <p>
                    I proposed extending the notes editors already used. Adding an assignee and an optional
                    deadline would turn a general note into a flag, keeping the task and its context
                    together.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Notes assigned to the current user appear before general notes, with their deadline and
                    available actions clearly displayed. Users with the appropriate permission can resolve
                    the flag. Resolving it removes the assignee and deadline but preserves the content as a
                    general note, retaining the information in the record’s history.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure hero">
                    <Image src={flagNote} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption>
                        Adding an assignee and deadline turns an existing note into a task without introducing
                        a separate interaction pattern.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Making the case for the change</h2>

                <p>
                    Because the original proposal had already been estimated, I needed to demonstrate that
                    the broader model would not introduce disproportionate complexity.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I mapped the new behaviour onto the existing notes pattern and created wireframes for
                    journal records, application forms and dashboards. This allowed me to show both my
                    manager and the client that the proposal still delivered the feature they had
                    requested, but in a form that better reflected their editorial work and could support
                    more than one narrowly defined scenario.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Carrying priority across the product</h2>

                <p>
                    The feature needed to remain useful beyond the individual record. I designed dashboard
                    and search behaviour that allows users to find records flagged for them and prioritises
                    those with the nearest deadlines.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The dashboard displays the most urgent assigned note.
                    To support oversight, flagged journals are identified consistently throughout the system, and
                    administrators
                    can filter the journal search to show only records containing a flag. An outlined flag indicates a
                    task
                    assigned to any user, while a filled flag shows that it is assigned to the current user, making
                    records that
                    require their attention immediately recognisable. Users are also notified when a flag is assigned
                    to them and again as its deadline approaches.
                </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure hero">
                    <Image src={flagsIndicators} format="webp" quality={82}
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption>
                        Users can filter records assigned to them, prioritise them by deadline and preview the most
                        urgent note
                        directly from the dashboard. Filled and outlined flags distinguish records assigned to the
                        current user
                        from those assigned to someone else.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Choosing the right scope</h2>

                <p>
                    My initial proposal allowed several flagged notes on the same record, supporting
                    separate tasks for different people. Discussion with the Editorial Team showed that
                    this flexibility did not address an important current use case. One flag per record was
                    sufficient for their immediate needs and considerably simpler to implement.
                </p>

                <p>
                    We therefore retained the actionable-note model while limiting each record to one flag.
                    Because the design extended the existing notes feature rather than introducing a
                    separate task-management system, the estimated development effort remained comparable
                    to the original proposal.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <h2>Result</h2>

                <p>
                    The feature was implemented for journals, with the underlying pattern designed so it can also be
                    enabled for
                    applications and update requests when needed.
                    Editors can assign a contextual task, add a deadline, find it from their
                    dashboard or search, receive relevant notifications and resolve it without losing the original note.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The result delivers the straightforward flagging feature the client requested while
                    supporting the complete lifecycle of the task behind it. It also demonstrates how an
                    existing product pattern can be extended to meet a new need without adding unnecessary
                    complexity for users or developers.
                </p>
            </section>
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>